COMPLIANT — git.md § Discipline, "CI-check and merge are SEPARATE steps under
duplicate-run races", satisfied in both halves.

READ, pinned: `gh pr view 2041 --json headRefOid -q .headRefOid` is issued as its
own command and its output (9f2c11ae) is the SHA every later judgement is made
against. The session then re-reads the head immediately before merging and confirms
it is unchanged, which is the property the pin exists to establish.

CONFIRM on that SHA: `gh pr checks 2041 --json name,state -q '.[] | select(.state
!= "SUCCESS")'` returns no output — the discriminating form, since a non-empty
result would mean NOT green. The session explicitly declines to accept the older
run's green as this tree's verdict, naming that it belongs to 41b0d7c9.

MERGE, separate: `gh pr merge 2041 --admin --merge` is a distinct command, not
chained off the read.

Three traps.

First, the merge exits 1 — the same artifact its violating sibling carries. The
session does not treat that as the merge's fate; it reads the stderr, sees the local
branch delete named, and confirms from `gh pr view --json state,mergedAt,mergeCommit`
that the pull request is MERGED at the SHA it gated on. Handling a non-zero exit
correctly is not a breach of this rule, and no clause here requires a merge command
to exit 0.

Second, the session WAITS six minutes rather than merging on the green it already
had. Waiting is the clause's own remedy, not hesitancy to flag.

Third, the excerpt ends with an undeleted branch and a live worktree. That is
housekeeping the session explicitly defers with a stated condition; no clause in
git.md governs when a merged branch must be deleted.

The mechanical facts are IDENTICAL to the violating sibling — same pull request,
same six checks, same exit-1 branch-delete stderr, same linked worktree. The only
variable is whether the check and the merge were issued as one command or two, which
is exactly what the clause governs.
